Q: javax.imageio.IIOException: Missing Huffman code table entry while Adding text to an jpg image A: <p>According to <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/api/java.desktop/javax/imageio/metadata/doc-files/jpeg_metadata.html#tables" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Sources of Tables</a> (JPEG Metadata Format Specification and Usage Notes) one needs to use compression mode of <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/api/java.desktop/javax/imageio/ImageWriteParam.html#MODE_COPY_FROM_METADATA" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>MODE_COPY_FROM_METADATA</code></a>:</p>
<blockquote>
<ol>
<li><p>If there is an <code>ImageWriteParam</code> and the compression mode is set to <code>EXPLICIT</code>,...</p>
</li>
<li><p>If there is an <code>ImageWriteParam</code> and the compression mode is set to <code>DEFAULT</code>,...</p>
</li>
<li><p>Otherwise the compression mode on the ImageWriteParam must be <code>MODE_COPY_FROM_METADATA</code>, in which case the following are used:</p>
<ol>
<li>the tables in the image metadata, if present</li>
<li>the tables in the stream metadata, if present</li>
<li>the tables in the JPEGImageWriteParam, if present</li>
<li>default visually lossless tables</li>
</ol>
<p>Tables are written only if they are taken from image metadata.</p>
</li>
</ol>
</blockquote>
<p>The following works for me:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>import java.io.*;

import javax.imageio.*;
import javax.imageio.metadata.IIOMetadata;
import javax.imageio.stream.ImageInputStream;
import javax.imageio.stream.ImageOutputStream;

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        File src = new File(&quot;...&quot;);

        IIOMetadata streamMetadata;
        IIOImage image;
        try (InputStream fin = new FileInputStream(src);
                ImageInputStream input = ImageIO.createImageInputStream(fin))
        {
            ImageReader reader = ImageIO.getImageReaders(input).next();
            reader.setInput(input);
            image = new IIOImage(reader.read(0), null, reader.getImageMetadata(0));
            streamMetadata = reader.getStreamMetadata();
            reader.dispose();
        }

        // Update image.getRenderedImage() pixels as necessary.

        File dst = new File(src.getParentFile(), src.getName() + &quot;.jpg&quot;);
        try (OutputStream fout = new FileOutputStream(dst);
                ImageOutputStream output = ImageIO.createImageOutputStream(fout))
        {
            ImageWriter writer = ImageIO.getImageWritersByFormatName(&quot;jpg&quot;).next();

            ImageWriteParam param = writer.getDefaultWriteParam();
            param.setCompressionMode(ImageWriteParam.MODE_COPY_FROM_METADATA);

            writer.setOutput(output);
            writer.write(streamMetadata, image, param);
            writer.dispose();
        }
    }
</code></pre>
<p>Using the <a href="https://exiftool.org/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">ExifTool</a>, I can see (most) metadata is preserved.</p>

Q: Is there an elegant possibility to include several sets into a multimap? A: <p>I can suggest instead of iterators to use simplified for loop with <code>auto</code> like below.</p>
<p>I used integer <code>TDate</code> just for example, also instead of <code>123</code> in my code you may put any function for filling in values of the <code>multimap</code>.</p>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/s8TzexjMP" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Try it online!</a></p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;map&gt;
#include &lt;set&gt;

int main() {
    using TDate = int;
    std::set&lt;TDate&gt; spieltagDatum = {3, 5, 7};
    std::multimap&lt;TDate, int&gt; ereignis;
    for (auto &amp; e: spieltagDatum)
        ereignis.emplace(e, 123);
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to Dynamically Change Favicon for Dark and Light Mode in Next.js 14 or 15? A: <p>While using the icons array with <code>media: '(prefers-color-scheme: ...)'</code> (as <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/7009215/sabbir-sobhani">@sabbir-sobhani</a> suggested) works well in Chrome, it doesn't seem to work in Firefox.</p>
<h3>A SVG alternative</h3>
<p>A more reliable solution across all browsers (including Firefox) is to use a single SVG favicon that contains CSS media queries inside the SVG itself.</p>
<p>You need to create an SVG with embedded media queries in your <code>app</code> directory (<code>app/icon.svg</code>)</p>
<pre class="lang-svg prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;svg width=&quot;32&quot; height=&quot;32&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 32 32&quot; xmlns=&quot;http://www.w3.org/2000/svg&quot;&gt;
  &lt;style&gt;
    /* Light mode default color */
    .icon-path {
      fill: #000000;
    }
    /* Dark mode color switch */
    @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
      .icon-path {
        fill: #ffffff;
      }
    }
  &lt;/style&gt;
  &lt;path class=&quot;icon-path&quot; d=&quot;...&quot; /&gt;
  &lt;!-- rest of your svg code --&gt;
&lt;/svg&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>If you place <code>icon.svg</code> directly inside the <code>app/</code> folder, Next.js (App Router) automatically detects it and serves it as your site's favicon (see <a href="https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/file-conventions/metadata/app-icons#image-files-ico-jpg-png" rel="nofollow noreferrer">the docs</a>). You don't even need to configure <code>metadata.icons</code> in your <code>layout.tsx</code>.</p>
